   └── MeetingNotesI am building a File Upload Web API using ASP.NET Core with Entity Framework Core and MySQL (running in Docker). The system contains three modules: BestPractice, LessonsLearned, and MeetingNotes. Each module allows users to upload a file along with additional metadata, and the file is stored in a server folder (wwwroot/UploadedFiles) while only file information such as FileName, FilePath, ContentType, FileSize, and UploadedDate is stored in the database. Each module has its own entity and table in MySQL, but all share a common file handling mechanism. The architecture follows a clean layered approach where Controllers handle HTTP requests, Services contain business logic, a single common FileService handles all file operations (save, delete, validate, and return metadata), Repositories handle database operations using DbContext, and EF Core connects to MySQL. The request flow starts from the client sending a multipart/form-data request to the controller, which calls the service layer, then the FileService stores the file in disk, and finally the service saves metadata into the database through the repository. GET requests retrieve stored metadata, and DELETE requests remove both database records and physical files. The system ensures separation of concerns, reusability, and clean architecture principles with one FileService shared across all modules and independent services and repositories for each entity.
